\section{System Model}\label{sec:sys}
\subsection{Scenario and signal model}
A BS with an $M$-antenna lens array transmits to $K$ single-antenna users through an RIS with $N$ elements (Fig.~\ref{fig:sys}). The direct BS--user link is blocked. The lens is modelled as an ideal unitary DFT $\mathbf U\in\mathbb C^{M\times M}$ whose $m$-th column is the beam toward $\sin\varphi_m=(2m-(M-1))/M$. Only the beams in a set $\mathcal S$ with $|\mathcal S|=N_s$ have an RF chain; $\mathbf U_{\mathcal S}$ collects the selected columns. With a beamspace precoder $\mathbf W\in\mathbb C^{N_s\times K}$ and symbols $\mathbf s\sim\CN(\mathbf 0,\mathbf I_K)$ the transmit vector is $\mathbf x=\mathbf U_{\mathcal S}\mathbf W\mathbf s$ with $\|\mathbf W\|_F^2=P_\mathrm{BS}$.

\begin{figure}[t]\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{figs/fig_sys.pdf}
\caption{System model: lens-antenna BS with beam selection, active RIS with per-element amplifiers, $K$ single-antenna users, blocked direct link.}\label{fig:sys}
\end{figure}

The signal arriving at RIS element $n$ is $u_n=[\mathbf G\mathbf x]_n+v_n$ with $v_n\sim\CN(0,\sigma_v^2)$ the amplifier-input noise. The RIS applies a unit-modulus phase $e^{j\theta_n}$ and amplifies with the memoryless Rapp characteristic \cite{rapp1991} (smoothness factor 2)
\begin{equation}
g(u)=\frac{p\,u}{\bigl(1+(p|u|/A)^{4}\bigr)^{1/4}},\label{eq:rapp}
\end{equation}
where $p$ is the small-signal amplitude gain (taken equal for all elements) and $A$ the output saturation amplitude, set as $A^2=P_\mathrm{RIS}/N$. The user signal is
\begin{equation}
\mathbf r=\mathbf F\,\diag(e^{j\boldsymbol\theta})\,g(\mathbf u)+\mathbf n,\qquad \mathbf n\sim\CN(\mathbf 0,\sigma^2\mathbf I_K),\label{eq:rx}
\end{equation}
with $\mathbf F\in\mathbb C^{K\times N}$ the RIS--user channel (row $\mathbf f_k$).

\subsection{Channels}
The BS--RIS channel is a sum of $L$ paths,
\begin{equation}
\mathbf G=\sqrt{\mathrm{PL}_G}\sum_{l=1}^{L}c_l\,\mathbf a_N(\psi_l)\mathbf a_M(\phi_l)^H,\label{eq:G}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf a_X(\omega)=[1,e^{j\pi\omega},\dots,e^{j\pi(X-1)\omega}]^T$ (half-wavelength spacing), $\sin$-domain angles $\phi_l,\psi_l\sim\mathcal U(-0.85,0.85)$, $\sum_l|c_l|^2=1$, path~1 carries 70\% of the power, the remaining 30\% is split by a symmetric Dirichlet draw, and the phases are uniform. Hence $\|\mathbf G\|_F^2\approx\mathrm{PL}_G NM$ (exact up to path cross-terms) with the free-space loss $\mathrm{PL}_G=(\lambda/4\pi d_{BR})^2$ and unit antenna gains. The RIS--user channel is line-of-sight: $\mathbf f_k=\sqrt{\mathrm{PL}_F}\,\gamma_k\,\mathbf a_N(\chi_k)^H$ with random phase $\gamma_k$, free-space loss $\mathrm{PL}_F$ at $d_{RU}$ and well-separated directions $\chi_k$. No shadowing or small-scale fading beyond this structure is modelled. Because $\operatorname{rank}\mathbf G\le L$,
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{rank}\bigl(\mathbf F\diag(\boldsymbol\kappa\odot e^{j\boldsymbol\theta})\mathbf G\mathbf U_{\mathcal S}\bigr)\le\min\{K,L,N_s\},\label{eq:rank}
\end{equation}
which is the origin of the rank limit studied in Section~\ref{sec:rank} (a standard linear-algebra bound, not claimed as new).

\subsection{Bussgang model of the amplifier}
For $u_n\sim\CN(0,P_n)$ with $P_n=\|\mathbf e_n^T\mathbf G\mathbf U_{\mathcal S}\mathbf W\|^2+\sigma_v^2$, Bussgang's theorem \cite{buss1952} gives $g(u_n)=\kappa_n u_n+d_n$, where $\kappa_n=\E[g(u_n)u_n^*]/P_n$ and the distortion $d_n$ is uncorrelated with $u_n$ and has power $\sigma_{d,n}^2=\E|g(u_n)|^2-\kappa_n^2P_n$. For the Rapp function, $\kappa_n$ and $\E|g(u_n)|^2$ are one-dimensional integrals over $t=|u_n|^2/P_n\sim\mathrm{Exp}(1)$ (Appendix); we evaluate them with a 96-point Gauss--Laguerre rule. With the usual approximation that distortion terms are independent across elements, the SINR of user $k$ is
\begin{equation}
\gamma_k=\frac{|T_{kk}|^2}{\sum_{j\ne k}|T_{kj}|^2+\sum_n|f_{kn}|^2\bigl(\kappa_n^2\sigma_v^2+\sigma_{d,n}^2\bigr)+\sigma^2},\label{eq:sinr}
\end{equation}
with $\mathbf T=\mathbf F\diag(\boldsymbol\kappa\odot e^{j\boldsymbol\theta})\mathbf G\mathbf U_{\mathcal S}\mathbf W$. The rate is $R_k=\log_2(1+\gamma_k)$ and the RIS output power is $P_\mathrm{out}=\sum_n\E|g(u_n)|^2\le P_\mathrm{RIS}$. The independence approximation and Gaussian input are checked against symbol-level simulation in Section~\ref{sec:val}.

\subsection{Power consumption}
The total consumption is
\begin{equation}
P_\mathrm{tot}=\frac{P_\mathrm{BS}}{\eta_\mathrm{BS}}+N_sP_\mathrm{RF}+P_\mathrm{fix}+\frac{P_\mathrm{out}}{\eta_\mathrm{RIS}}+N P_\mathrm{el},\label{eq:power}
\end{equation}
with $P_\mathrm{el}=P_\mathrm{el}^\mathrm{act}$ for the active RIS (amplifier bias per element) and, for the passive reference, $P_\mathrm{out}$ is omitted and $P_\mathrm{el}=P_\mathrm{el}^\mathrm{pas}$. Energy efficiency is $\mathrm{EE}=\sum_kR_k/P_\mathrm{tot}$ in bit/s/Hz/W. The constants (Table~\ref{tab:par}) are ranges from the literature or explicit assumptions; Section~\ref{sec:ee} tests their influence.

\section{Design Procedure}\label{sec:des}
\subsection{Problem and approach}
For a given user set and $N_s$ the goal is to maximise $\sum_kR_k$ over $(\mathcal S,\mathbf W,p,\boldsymbol\theta)$ subject to $|\mathcal S|=N_s$, $\|\mathbf W\|_F^2\le P_\mathrm{BS}$, $P_\mathrm{out}\le P_\mathrm{RIS}$ and $|e^{j\theta_n}|=1$. This problem is non-convex and we do not solve it to optimality. The procedure below is a heuristic pipeline; $N_s$ is swept rather than optimised, and EE is evaluated afterwards.

\textbf{Phase gradient.} For fixed $\mathbf W$ and $p$ the powers $P_n$, hence $\kappa_n$ and $\sigma_{d,n}^2$, do not depend on $\boldsymbol\theta$. Writing $c_{kj}[n]=f_{kn}\kappa_n[\mathbf G\mathbf U_{\mathcal S}\mathbf W]_{nj}$, \eqref{eq:sinr} becomes
\begin{equation}
\gamma_k=\frac{|\mathbf c_{kk}^T e^{j\boldsymbol\theta}|^2}{\sum_{j\ne k}|\mathbf c_{kj}^Te^{j\boldsymbol\theta}|^2+\nu_k},
\end{equation}
so the objective and its gradient with respect to $\boldsymbol\theta$ are available in closed form. We ascend the proportional-fair objective $\sum_k\log_2(R_k+\epsilon)$, $\epsilon=0.5$, with Adam \cite{kingma2015}; plain sum-rate maximisation tended to starve weak users in earlier trials.

\begin{algorithm}[t]\caption{Design for one user group (one time slot)}\label{alg:design}\small
\KwIn{channels $\mathbf G,\mathbf F$ of the group, $N_s$, $P_\mathrm{BS}$, $P_\mathrm{RIS}$}
Align RIS phases with a ZF precoder over all $M$ beams and rank the beams by cascaded energy\;
\eIf{$N_s\ge 25$}{$\mathcal S\leftarrow$ all $M$ beams (full-digital reference)}{greedy forward selection on the \emph{linear}-amplifier model: start from the $K$ strongest beams; add, from the $\max\{24,2N_s\}$ strongest, the beam that maximises the sum-rate (8-point $p$ grid) until $|\mathcal S|=N_s$}
Initialise $\boldsymbol\theta$ by alternating phase alignment on $\mathcal S$\;
\For{outer round $=1,2$}{
 choose $(p,\alpha)$ on a grid ($p\in[1,100]$, 16 log-spaced points; $\alpha\in\{0,0.1,0.3,1\}$) maximising the sum-rate of the RZF precoder \cite{peel2005} built on the effective channel including $\kappa_n$ (two fixed-point updates), subject to $P_\mathrm{out}\le P_\mathrm{RIS}$\;
 refine $\boldsymbol\theta$: 40 Adam steps on the proportional-fair objective with $\mathbf W,p$ fixed\;
 keep the design with the best objective\;}
\KwOut{$\mathcal S,\mathbf W,p,\boldsymbol\theta$}\end{algorithm}

\subsection{User grouping}
When $K$ exceeds what the channel rank supports, users are split into $\lceil K/g\rceil$ groups served in equal time slots, so each user's rate is $R_k/\lceil K/g\rceil$. Groups are built greedily: the strongest remaining user (largest $\|\mathbf f_k\|$) starts a group and users with the smallest maximum normalised correlation $|\mathbf f_i\mathbf f_j^H|/(\|\mathbf f_i\|\|\mathbf f_j\|)$ to current members are added until the group holds $g$ users. Algorithm~\ref{alg:design} runs separately in each slot, and $P_\mathrm{tot}$ is averaged over slots. We report the sum-rate, the minimum user rate, Jain's index \cite{jain1984} and EE, all time-averaged.

\subsection{Complexity}
The $K\times K\times N$ tensor of $c_{kj}[n]$ is formed once per refinement call and each Adam step then costs $\mathcal O(K^2N)$. One evaluation of \eqref{eq:sinr} costs $\mathcal O(NN_sK)$ plus $N$ one-dimensional quadratures; each greedy step evaluates the grid design for up to $\max\{24,2N_s\}$ candidates, so beam selection dominates for $N_s\le24$. We make no low-complexity claim: the implementation is unoptimised Python and the whole set of configurations for one scene takes a median @@sec4@@~s, @@sec8@@~s and @@sec16@@~s for $K=4,8,16$ on a shared two-core CPU (the maximum was @@sec16max@@~s for $K=16$). Full-digital designs avoid the greedy search, so run-time is not comparable between arms.

