\section{Results}\label{sec:res}
\subsection{Validation of the Bussgang model}\label{sec:val}
For @@val_n@@ designs (ten draws each for $(K,L,N_s)=$ $(4,6,12)$, $(8,16,12)$ and $(16,16,24)$) we compared the analytical sum-rate with a symbol-level simulation that passes Gaussian symbols through \eqref{eq:rapp} (60\,000 symbols per design, SINR from the empirical correlation with the transmitted symbols). The mean absolute difference was @@val_mean_abs@@~bit/s/Hz (@@val_mean_rel_pct@@\% relative), the maximum @@val_max_abs@@~bit/s/Hz (@@val_max_rel_pct@@\%), and the analytical rate was on average @@val_signed@@~bit/s/Hz \emph{higher}. Reported analytical rates are therefore slightly optimistic. The comparison does not cover non-Gaussian (finite-alphabet) inputs.

\subsection{Rank limit and user grouping}\label{sec:rank}
Fig.~\ref{fig:svd} shows the singular values of $\mathbf G$ for draws 0--19 (the first 20 seeds of the grid). With a $-20$~dB threshold, the median effective rank is @@er6@@ for $L=6$ and @@er16@@ for $L=16$, i.e.\ well below $K=16$, consistent with \eqref{eq:rank}.

\begin{figure}[t]\centering\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{figs/fig_svd.pdf}\caption{Singular values of $\mathbf G$ (median of 20 draws, $-20$~dB rank threshold used in the text).}\label{fig:svd}\end{figure}

Fig.~\ref{fig:group} and Table~\ref{tab:main} show the effect on $K=16$ users. For $L=16$, the best group size among $\{4,8,16\}$ is $g=8$: with $N_s=1.5g$ the sum-rate is @@S_16_8@@~bit/s/Hz versus @@S_16_16@@ for serving all users together, and the minimum user rate rises from @@Mn_16_16@@ to @@Mn_16_8@@~bit/s/Hz. Taking the best $N_s$ for each group size, the best sum-rates are @@best_16_4@@, @@best_16_8@@ and @@best_16_16@@~bit/s/Hz for $g=4,8,16$. For $L=6$ the best group size is $g=4$, with @@best_6_4@@, @@best_6_8@@ and @@best_6_16@@~bit/s/Hz for $g=4,8,16$ (best $N_s$ each). In this channel the best $g$ is just below the effective rank; only three values of $g$ were tested, so we do not claim a closed-form rule.

For $L=6$, however, fairness remains poor even with the proportional-fair phase objective: at $g=4$ the average minimum user rate is only @@Mn_6_4@@~bit/s/Hz and Jain's index @@J_6_4@@. Part of the sum-rate benefit therefore comes from favouring users with strong channels, and the grouping rule uses only RIS-side correlations and norms (no rate feedback).

