\subsection{Energy efficiency versus number of RF chains}\label{sec:ee}
Fig.~\ref{fig:ee} plots EE and sum-rate for $g=4$ against $N_s$. EE peaks at few RF chains: the best EE among the tested active-RIS configurations at $K=16$ is @@ee16_16@@~bit/s/Hz/W for $L=16$ (@@ee16_16_cfg@@) and @@ee16_6@@ for $L=6$ (@@ee16_6_cfg@@), whereas $g=4$ with all 64 beams reaches only @@ee64_g4_16@@ ($L=16$) and @@ee64_g4_6@@ ($L=6$) because the total power rises from @@P12@@ to @@P64@@~W. Going from $N_s=12$ to 64 at $g=4$ raises the sum-rate by only @@gain64_16@@\% ($L=16$) and @@gain64_6@@\% ($L=6$), so the additional chains mostly add power.

\begin{figure*}[t]\centering\includegraphics[width=0.78\textwidth]{figs/fig_ee.pdf}\caption{EE (left) and sum-rate (right) versus the number of RF chains for $g=4$ ($N_s=64$ is full-digital). Mean with 95\% CI.}\label{fig:ee}\end{figure*}

Table~\ref{tab:ratio} gives the paired EE ratio between the best beamspace configuration and the full-digital configuration with the same group size, over all draws and group sizes in $\{4,8,K\}$ ($@@npairs@@$ pairs): mean @@EEr_mean@@$\times$, minimum @@EEr_min@@$\times$, maximum @@EEr_max@@$\times$. Because the best $N_s$ is chosen per draw, this ratio is an optimistic ``oracle'' figure; for fixed configurations the paired ratios (no per-draw selection, vs.\ $N_s=64$ at the same $g$) are @@fx_g4n6@@$\times$ ($g=4$, $N_s=6$), @@fx_g4n12@@$\times$ ($g=4$, $N_s=12$, minimum @@fx_g4n12_min@@) and @@fx_g8n12@@$\times$ ($g=8$, $N_s=12$, minimum @@fx_g8n12_min@@). The mean sum-rate loss of the best-EE beamspace configuration relative to full-digital is @@loss_mean@@\% (maximum @@loss_max@@\% in a single draw). That a 64-beam design is on average barely better than a few-beam subset suggests that our heuristic does not exploit the extra beams (in the ideal case a full-digital design can never be worse than a beam subset). The EE ratios are therefore partly a property of our design procedure and should not be read as guaranteed hardware gains.

