\subsection{Passive RIS reference}
With the same BS power and a passive RIS ($p=1$) the average sum-rate at $K=8$, $L=16$, $g=4$, $N_s=12$ is @@pas_K8L16@@~bit/s/Hz versus @@act_K8L16@@ for the active RIS; across all $(K,L)$ draws it never exceeds @@pas_max@@~bit/s/Hz (Table~\ref{tab:main} gives the $K=16$ case). This large gap reflects the chosen link budget (blocked direct link, 1~W BS, $30$ m total reflected path) and the fact that the active RIS adds up to 0.1~W of output power; the EE column accounts for the extra consumption, but we did not evaluate the passive RIS with a larger BS power or more RIS elements. The comparison is scenario-specific.

\subsection{Saturation and beam selection}\label{sec:dec}
Because the beam selection used in Section~\ref{sec:rank}--\ref{sec:ee} ranks beams with a linear amplifier, we tested whether this ordering matters. For @@dec_draws@@ channel draws ($K=4$, $L=6$, $P_\mathrm{RIS}=10$~dBm in this diagnostic), saturation back-offs $\beta=10\log_{10}(A^2N/P_\mathrm{RIS})\in\{-5,0,5\}$~dB and 120 random sets of six beams among the 20 strongest, we compared the sum-rate ordering under a linear amplifier and under the saturating amplifier. The Spearman correlation was lowest, @@dec_rho_min@@, at $\beta=-5$~dB and at least @@dec_rho_p5@@ at $\beta=+5$~dB; the best beam set under the linear model coincided with the best one under saturation in @@dec_same@@ of @@dec_n@@ cases, and the largest rate loss from using the linear-model choice was @@dec_maxloss@@~bit/s/Hz. Saturation therefore has a modest influence on which beams to select when RIS input power is spread by about @@dec_spread_lo@@--@@dec_spread_hi@@~dB across elements. This test used random beam sets and $K=4$ only; it does not exclude a larger interaction for other geometries or stronger compression.

\section{Discussion and Limitations}\label{sec:lim}
\textbf{What the results support.} (i) Under a blocked direct link and a few resolvable BS--RIS paths, the number of simultaneously served users is bounded by the effective channel rank, and time-shared groups of comparable size raise the average sum-rate and EE. (ii) In our power model, EE is maximised with 6--12 RF chains rather than 64. (iii) The ordering of beams is only weakly sensitive to amplifier saturation in the tested cases.

\textbf{What they do not support.} No claim is made about optimality, about superiority over joint-optimisation algorithms (none was implemented as a baseline), or about scaling beyond $K=16$ ($K=32$ was dropped for computing cost). Results are for one geometry (20~m/10~m, free space), idealised channel and lens models (no blockage on the RIS--user side, no beam-squint, no lens loss), perfect CSI, and uniform amplifier gain across RIS elements. The designs are heuristics; sum-rate is not monotonic in $N_s$ in our results, which shows the pipeline leaves performance unrealised. Several power constants ($\eta_\mathrm{BS}$, $\eta_\mathrm{RIS}$, $P_\mathrm{fix}$, $P_\mathrm{el}^\mathrm{pas}$) are assumptions; the best $N_s$ per draw is an oracle choice. Rates are analytical (slightly optimistic, Section~\ref{sec:val}), channel draws are @@n@@ per setting, and time-sharing assumes ideal slot allocation with no signalling or switching overhead.

\section{Conclusion}
For a lens-antenna BS serving many users through an active RIS with a blocked direct link, the achievable multiplexing is governed by the effective rank of the BS--RIS channel rather than by the number of users or RF chains. In our simulations, grouping users close to that rank and using a few RF chains gave the best sum-rate/EE trade-off, with EE gains over a 64-RF-chain design of about @@fx_g4n12@@$\times$ for fixed $g=4$, $N_s=12$ and between @@sens_lo@@ and @@sens_hi@@$\times$ over the tested power constants (best-per-draw). These figures depend on our heuristic design, assumed constants and a single geometry, so they indicate regimes rather than guarantees. Natural next steps are joint optimisation of beams, precoder, phases and non-uniform amplifier gains under the saturation model, $K$ beyond 16, imperfect CSI, and measured lens/amplifier characteristics.

